<header class="sp-head"><a class="sp-portrait" href="/encyclopedia/boudica" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boudica.jpg" alt="" width="104" height="104"></a><p class="featured-region">60 or 61 CE · Roman Britain</p><h1 class="sp-title">Boudica before the Final Battle</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boudica">Boudica</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>When Prasutagus, king of the Iceni in eastern Britain, died, Roman officials seized his kingdom, flogged his widow Boudica and raped their daughters. Boudica led the Iceni and their neighbours in a revolt that destroyed Camulodunum, Londinium and Verulamium. As the governor Suetonius Paulinus brought her army to battle, Tacitus has her ride along the ranks in a chariot with her daughters.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>This is a woman's resolve; as for men, they may live and be slaves.</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">Tacitus, Annals, XIV.35, tr. A. J. Church and W. J. Brodribb (1876). Tacitus wrote some fifty years later, and the words are his.</p><div class="sp-full"><button type="button" class="sp-share" data-share-text="“This is a woman's resolve; as for men, they may live and be slaves.” — Boudica, Boudica before the Final Battle" data-share-img="/images/og/speech-boudica-before-the-battle.jpg">Share this quote</button></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec sp-ft" id="full-text"><h2>The full speech</h2><div class="sp-ft-body"><p>Boudicea, with her daughters before her in a chariot, went up to tribe after tribe, protesting that it was indeed usual for Britons to fight under the leadership of women. &quot;But now,&quot; she said, &quot;it is not as a woman descended from noble ancestry, but as one of the people that I am avenging lost freedom, my scourged body, the outraged chastity of my daughters. Roman lust has gone so far that not our very persons, nor even age or virginity, are left unpolluted. But heaven is on the side of a righteous vengeance; a legion which dared to fight has perished; the rest are hiding themselves in their camp, or are thinking anxiously of flight. They will not sustain even the din and the shout of so many thousands, much less our charge and our blows. If you weigh well the strength of the armies, and the causes of the war, you will see that in this battle you must conquer or die. This is a woman's resolve; as for men, they may live and be slaves.&quot;</p></div><p class="sp-ft-credit">Text: <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Annals_(Tacitus)/Book_14" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>, tr. A. J. Church and W. J. Brodribb (1876). The text is in the public domain; the Wikisource transcription is shared under CC BY-SA 4.0.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>The Romans, though heavily outnumbered, won a crushing victory at an unknown site, probably in the Midlands, and Tacitus reports that Boudica took poison. Rome kept its province, but Paulinus was soon replaced by a more conciliatory governor. Remembered again once Tacitus was rediscovered in the Renaissance, Boudica became a heroine of British national legend.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-conquest-of-britain">Roman conquest of Britain</a></div></div></section>
